<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Quelimane</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Quelimane is a port town on the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a>, near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambezi">Zambezi</a> delta. In the eighteenth century gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwean-plateau">Zimbabwean plateau</a> reached the coast through Quelimane and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofala">Sofala</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rozvi-empire">Rozvi Empire</a> expelled the Portuguese from the interior, trade flowed increasingly through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili merchants</a> operating in these ports.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/quelimane.jpg" alt="Quelimane" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: A Verdade from Maputo, Moçambique · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cine_Teatro_Aguia_Quelimane_(9516168590).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…terms: Portuguese and Afro-Portuguese merchants were confined to licensed fairs such as Zumbo and Masekesa on the margins of Rozvi territory, and the gold and ivory trade passed through Sena and Tete to the coast at <b>Quelimane</b> and Sofala under African rather than European control of the interior.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rozvi-empire">Rozvi Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Lundu controlled routes carrying ivory from the interior to the coast near <b>Quelimane</b> and Mozambique Island and drew tribute from Manganja chiefs.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-lundu">Kingdom of Lundu</a></li></ul></section>
